## 5. Evaluation

### 5.1 In-domain commonsense (utility)
The 8-dataset multiple-choice accuracy eval used by MiLoRA/DoRA (LLM-Adapters protocol), last checkpoint. **[VERIFY]** you're using the same harness CLoRA used — the gate is reproducing LoRA ≈79.9 avg. Do **not** substitute lm-eval likelihood-MC here; the numbers won't match.

### 5.2 Out-domain (retention)
BBH + MMLU-Pro via **lm-eval**. **[VERIFY]** exact task names and few-shot settings from the CLoRA repo; the gate is reproducing base 34.91/18.56.

### 5.3 Mechanism metric F∆ (cheap, high-value — add it)
From CLoRA Table 4, define per updating matrix ΔW and input x:
`F∆(ΔW, x) = ‖ΔW x‖ / ‖x‖`, averaged over **100 real-world inputs**, over all tokens and all updated matrices. Lower = less output disruption = less forgetting. Also report `‖ΔW‖` (largest singular value = capacity proxy).

Targets to reproduce (Table 4, rank 32, CS): LoRA F∆=0.79, ‖ΔW‖=22.63; CLoRA-k2048 F∆=0.14, ‖ΔW‖=5.00. This metric is nearly free (one 100-input forward pass + a singular-value call per matrix) and gives the mechanism-level story: *UIOrthoLoRA should achieve low F∆ by construction (orthogonal to the protected directions), where CLoRA achieves it via a tuned penalty.* Compute it for LoRA, best-CLoRA, best-UIOrthoLoRA. **[VERIFY]** the exact averaging convention (over tokens and matrices) and the "reference row" definition from the repo.

**Phase 1 uses CLoRA's native metrics only.** Do not bring in the negative-shift / error-set framework here — keep the go/no-go legible and directly comparable to CLoRA's tables. The dual-axis framework is a later, supplementary layer.

